# Michimono design system

Michicast is a Michimono product. The house style — neutrals, type, shape, motifs — is shared across products; only the house stops and the product accent change per product. Never restyle a screen with ad-hoc values; extend the tokens or the components below.

## The locked ramp

**Wanderer duo.** Canopy fuchsia `#FE7AF8` → forest blue `#5B8AD4`, accent navy `#2E5490`, lift mist `#89B6D5`. Settled by the product owner (issue #76). Do not substitute a stop, add a third, or re-pick; propose a change to the human instead.

Two stops, not three, and the same two in both themes. Cool mist paper and navy ink replace the retired Sherbet cream ramp. The night theme therefore flips surfaces and swaps the accent pair; the signature itself never changes, which is the whole point of having one.

Sherbet (flamingo → periwinkle on warm cream) is retired. Do not reintroduce its stops, butter as a third stop, or warm cream paper.

Rejected along the way, so nobody re-proposes them: Marmalade, Koi and Neon citrus (a dark stop inside the ramp, or a stop that fights the delivery states); Sakura dusk, Ukiyo sunset, Kingyo and Matcha momo (warm middles crowd the failure red, or weak accents); Seiji, which was briefly the night palette until one ramp for both themes proved stronger; deepening butter to marigold `#E9A020`; and Sherbet once Wanderer became the house look.

## Contrast floors

The gradient is decoration and never carries body text — neither stop reaches the 4.5:1 that small text needs on paper. Display-size lettering, marks, rules, tracks and borders only. Readable text uses `ink` on paper or `accent` for links; on ink it is `ink-contrast` and the dark `accent`. Any new colour must be measured against the surface it lands on before it goes in.

## Chrome is light

The header is a white bar with a hairline bottom border, the mark in its original black, and quiet grey navigation. Do not reintroduce dark chrome, an ink header band, or an inverted mark on light screens — it was tried and rejected. Darkness arrives only with the night theme, applied to whole surfaces rather than as a decorative band.

## Tokens, never raw values

All tokens live in `@theme` in `resources/css/app.css`. In Blade, use the generated utilities (`bg-paper`, `text-ink`, `border-line`, `text-accent`, `font-display`, `rounded-card`). Do not write `zinc-*`, `gray-*`, another Tailwind palette, a hex code, or an arbitrary colour in a view. If a needed value is missing, add a token rather than a one-off.

- Surfaces: `paper` (page), `paper-raised` (cards, inputs), `ink` (chrome), `ink-raised`.
- Text: `ink`, `muted`, and on dark chrome `ink-contrast`, `ink-muted`.
- Strokes: `line`, `line-strong`. Borders are structural, never decorative.
- Product accent: `accent` for links, primary buttons and focus rings; `accent-lift` is its counterpart on the opposite surface. The pair swaps under `.dark`, so `accent` always means "the interactive colour that reads here".
- House stops: `house-1`, `house-2`, composed as `--house-gradient`. Reach for the gradient, not the stops; nothing should use a single stop as a flat colour.

## Type roles

- `font-display` (Instrument Serif) — the wordmark, page and card headings. Weight 400 only; the serif *is* the emphasis, so do not bold it.
- `font-sans` (Instrument Sans) — all interface text.
- `font-mono` (IBM Plex Mono) — ids, destinations, URLs, timestamps, cron expressions, and the small uppercase kicker. Anything a person might copy or compare character by character belongs in mono.

## Gradient placement is a closed list

The house gradient is a signature, not a surface treatment. It is allowed only in these places, via the `house-*` utilities:

- `house-mark` — the logo filled with the gradient, and only where the mark needs to pop: empty states, auth screens, marketing. Chrome uses the flat mark.
- `house-underline` — the active navigation item.
- `house-fill` — the kicker rule beside a card heading, status rings, outlined pills, progress bars and sparklines.
- `house-text` — one hero figure per screen at most.
- `house-edge` — the border of an empty state. Set `--house-edge-surface` when it sits on anything but `paper-raised`.

Anything else stays flat. In particular: no gradient page backgrounds, no gradient header bands, no gradient buttons (primary buttons use the solid accent), and no gradient focus rings. Never place the gradient behind body text.

## Delivery state colour is information, not decoration

`state-ok` (emerald), `state-skip` (slate), `state-fail` (crimson) report what happened to content. They are never the brand accent and never the gradient, and colour alone never carries the meaning — always pair it with the word. `skipped` is deliberately neutral slate, not amber: skipping an item is an ordinary outcome, not a warning.

## The cat earns its keep, then gets out of the way

The mark is a cat and the product is allowed to know that, in places where nothing is at stake.

- `<x-icons.paw>` rides on primary buttons and tilts on hover.
- `<x-whisker-divider>` separates sections, drawn from the face mark.
- `<x-card eared>` puts small ears on the *primary* card of a screen — one card, never several.
- `<x-empty-state>` shows the sitting cat and one dry line.
- Paw trails are the approved pattern for progress once runs exist: a row of `<x-icons.paw>`, the completed ones filled from `--house-gradient`.

## Voice: playful where nothing is at stake, literal where something is

Play is allowed in empty states, success notes, onboarding, loading and 404s — "Nothing to chase yet". Errors, validation messages, delivery logs, credential screens and timestamps stay literal and specific — "Telegram rejected the message: chat not found". People read those when something is broken, and a joke there costs trust. Never make the user decode a pun to understand what failed.

## Brand assets

`public/images/brand/logo-day-{full,face,mark}.svg`, served directly and rendered through `<x-brand.mark>`:

- `face` (2:1) — header lockup.
- `mark` (square) — favicon and small scales.
- `cat` / `full` (tall) — empty states and hero moments.

The art is monochrome, so ink-on-paper needs no processing and cream-on-ink uses `invert`. When night variants arrive they land as `logo-night-*.svg` and only `<x-brand.mark>` changes.

## Dark theme

Not implemented, but the colours are decided (same ramp, swapped accent pair, lifted states — see the `.dark` block) and the variant strategy is set: `dark` is a class variant, declared with `@custom-variant` in `app.css`, not `prefers-color-scheme`. Use `dark:` utilities when the theme lands; never mix in a media-query approach alongside it. `ink-contrast` and `ink-muted` exist for text on dark surfaces.
